---
title: md
summary: A Markdown string, rendered at build time through your app's own markdown pipeline (the same remark/rehype plugins and highlighted fences) and inlined as a static region, for prose that lives in a component.
related:
  - macros/code
  - content/collections
  - macros/overview
---

`import.meta.og.md(text)` renders a Markdown string **at build time** and inlines it as a static region. It runs the text through your app's own markdown pipeline, with the same remark and rehype plugins and the same Shiki fences, so `md('# Hello')` in a component renders exactly like the same Markdown in a `.md` document.

```svelte
<script>
  import { Region } from 'ogygia';

  const intro = import.meta.og.md(`
    ## Getting started

    Install, then add the plugin. A fenced block highlights too:

    \`\`\`ts
    export const csr = false;
    \`\`\`
  `);
</script>

<Region of={intro} />
```

Because `md()` runs your document pipeline, it handles everything that pipeline does, including headings with generated ids, lists, emphasis, links and highlighted code fences. The result is a region, rendered with `<Region of={…} />`, and it ships as HTML with no client JS.

## The rules

- **`text` is a static string or template literal.** A `${…}` interpolation is a build error, because it is a runtime value the build cannot resolve. Dedent is automatic, as it is in [`code`](/docs/macros/code).
- **Static prose only.** `md()` is for prose and fenced code. If the Markdown compiles to something dynamic (an island import, a `<script>`, a component tag, a Svelte expression), the build fails, because a region made this way is static. For live, interactive content, use a real component, or a `.svx` collection, where islands are allowed.

## code vs md

The two are closely related. [`code`](/docs/macros/code) renders one highlighted snippet with a language and fence meta, while `md` renders a whole Markdown document with headings, lists, prose and fences inside it. Use `code` when you have exactly one block to show and `md` when you have a passage. Both return a region, so they render the same way.
